  obstruction for this door once the kernel is localized (consistent with Leg-B S4).

================================================================================================
[F] FLRW: the clock's charge redshifts as dust; the mediator's Gauss law forbids the charged homogeneous background
================================================================================================
  [PASS] clock charge: d_m(sqrt(-g) n u^m) = 0 on FLRW  =>  n = C a^{-3} (dust-like, the CMB-relevant scaling)
  [PASS] MUTATION CONTROL: a non-conserved current (source s) is NOT a^{-3}
  [PASS] homogeneous-isotropic mediator ansatz has F_mn == 0 identically (any A_0(t) is pure gauge)
  the nu=0 mediator equation on FLRW:  d_m[ sqrt(-g) (1/2piG) F_M' F^{m0} ] = q sqrt(-g) J^0 = q a^3 n
  nu=0 equation on the homogeneous ansatz: Eq(0, n_hom*q*a(t)**3)  -> n = [0]
  [PASS] Gauss law on the homogeneous background: the only solution is n = 0 (contradiction with the a^{-3} charge, unless it vanishes)
  [PASS] even an ANISOTROPIC homogeneous mediator (E_i(t) != 0, Bianchi-I-like) gives d_i E^i = 0 => n = 0: no homogeneous charged background at all
  periodic box: [flux]_0^L = 0   vs  INT q nbar dx = L_box*nbar*q
  [PASS] periodic-box Gauss identity: the net flux of the exponential-kernel field vanishes for a periodic V, the charge integral q nbar L does not
  [PASS] MUTATION CONTROL: a NEUTRALIZED source (n - nbar, zero mean) is consistent with Gauss -- but then the mean charge that was to give Omega_dm sources nothing
  [PASS] contrast: a SCALAR mediator (kernel on n, not J^m) has a homogeneous solution phi(t)  => the Gauss-law kill is specific to the vector/current kernel
  => the CMB pass (Omega_dm from the charge) is NOT automatic: with the massless vector mediator the charged
     homogeneous background does not exist.  Escapes: neutralize (then the mean charge sources no MOND potential),
     or a Proca mass (3 DOF, Yukawa cutoff must exceed Mpc), or a scalar mediator (a SECOND scalar: Case 4, and
     it still fails gate (a) for baryons by the [B] source-order argument, which is mediator-independent).

